## Why This Is Asked

This question explores advanced incremental data-structure design under streaming constraints, requiring mastery of amortized analysis for batched updates alongside exact substring query capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- **Suffix Automaton fundamentals**: States, transitions, links, and the endpos equivalence relation
- **Batched extension strategies**: Buffering B characters before committing to the global structure
- **Clone management and amortized cost**: Deferred clone creation and lazy propagation techniques
- **Transition representations**: Compact maps vs. dense arrays for cache locality
- **Trade-off analysis**: Balancing batch size B, alphabet size σ, and total string length N

## Code Example

```cpp
struct State {
    int link;
    int len;
    std::unordered_map<char, int> next;  // or vector<int> for small σ
};

class BatchedSuffixAutomaton {
    std::vector<State> st;
    int last;
    std::string buffer;
    const size_t B;

public:
    BatchedSuffixAutomaton(size_t batch_size = 1024) : B(batch_size), last(0) {
        st.push_back({-1, 0, {}});
    }

    void extend(char c) {
        buffer.push_back(c);
        if (buffer.size() < B) return;
        flushBuffer();
    }

    void flushBuffer() {
        for (char c : buffer) {
            int cur = st.size();
            st.push_back({0, st[last].len + 1, {}});
            int p = last;
            while (p >= 0 && !st[p].next.count(c)) {
                st[p].next[c] = cur;
                p = st[p].link;
            }
            if (p == -1) {
                st[cur].link = 0;
            } else {
                int q = st[p].next[c];
                if (st[p].len + 1 == st[q].len) {
                    st[cur].link = q;
                } else {
                    int clone = st.size();
                    st.push_back(st[q]);  // clone
                    st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
                    while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] == q) {
                        st[p].next[c] = clone;
                        p = st[p].link;
                    }
                    st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
                }
            }
            last = cur;
        }
        buffer.clear();
    }

    bool query(const std::string& p) {
        int v = 0;
        for (char c : p) {
            if (!st[v].next.count(c)) return false;
            v = st[v].next[c];
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

## Optimal Batch Size

The optimal B depends on workload characteristics:
- **Small σ (e.g., DNA, σ ≤ 4)**: Larger B (4096+) works well; dense transition arrays gain from cache locality
- **Large σ (e.g., Unicode, σ ≈ 10⁵)**: Smaller B (128–512); sparse maps reduce memory pressure
- **Memory-constrained environments**: B ≈ √N balances update frequency against per-batch overhead
- **Latency-sensitive queries**: Smaller B reduces worst-case query delay between flushes

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design streaming top-k algorithms within a sliding window using compact probabilistic data structures and articulate clear trade-offs between accuracy, space, and time.

## Key Concepts

- **Sliding window processing**: Maintaining state only over the most recent W elements
- **Space-Saving sketch**: A fixed-memory algorithm that provides approximate frequency counts
- **Top-k aggregation**: Identifying the k most frequent items in a stream
- **Approximation guarantees**: Bounding error in frequency estimates
- **Time/space complexity**: Analyzing per-operation and batch performance

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingTopK {
  constructor(k, W) {
    this.k = k;
    this.W = W;
    this.counters = new Map(); // item -> count
    this.window = []; // queue of last W items
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      const minEntry = this.findMinCounter();
      this.counters.delete(minEntry.key);
      this.counters.set(x, minEntry.value + 1);
    }
    this.window.push(x);
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      this.expire(this.window.shift());
    }
  }

  expire(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      const newCount = this.counters.get(x) - 1;
      if (newCount > 0) {
        this.counters.set(x, newCount);
      } else {
        this.counters.delete(x);
      }
    }
  }

  findMinCounter() {
    let minEntry = { key: null, value: Infinity };
    for (const [key, value] of this.counters) {
      if (value < minEntry.value) {
        minEntry = { key, value };
      }
    }
    return minEntry;
  }

  topK() {
    return [...this.counters.entries()]
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .slice(0, this.k);
  }
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to balance space, accuracy, and latency for streaming distinct counts under a sliding window constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Counting Bloom Filter with aging counters
- Sliding window semantics and estimation bias
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs: probabilistic versus exact data structures

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCBF {
  constructor(m, k, windowSize) {
    this.counters = new Array(m).fill(0);
    this.ringBuffer = new Array(windowSize);
    this.head = 0;
    this.k = k;
  }

  hash(id, j) {
    const h = (id * 31 + j * 17) % m;
    return Math.abs(h);
  }

  add(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]++;
    }
    this.ringBuffer[this.head] = id;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.ringBuffer.length;
  }

  remove(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]--;
    }
  }

  estimateDistinct() {
    return this.counters.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / this.k;
  }
}
```

